/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `lessons` DISABLE KEYS */;
INSERT INTO `lessons` VALUES (1,1,1,'reading','AI in your pocket','Many phone apps already use AI. Your bank or mobile money service may flag unusual transactions. Your keyboard predicts the next word. Maps suggest the fastest route. Translation apps turn Swahili into English in seconds.\n\nAI is software that finds patterns in large amounts of examples, then uses those patterns to make predictions or suggestions. It does not think like a person. It is very good at narrow tasks and can fail in surprising ways.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(2,1,2,'reading','How machines learn from data','Traditional software follows rules that a programmer writes. A machine learning system is different: it is shown many examples and works out the rules itself.\n\n## Training data\nIf you show a system thousands of photos labelled \"maize\" or \"cassava\", it can learn to tell them apart. The examples are called training data.\n\n## Garbage in, garbage out\nBad, missing or one-sided data produces bad results. If the examples mostly come from one region or group, the system may work poorly for everyone else.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(3,1,3,'discussion','Spot the AI','Think of one app or service you used this week that probably uses AI. What does it predict or suggest? Share it in the course discussion and read two other answers.',NULL,NULL,NULL,5,0),(4,2,1,'reading','Asking good questions (prompts)','The quality of an AI answer depends on the quality of your request. A good prompt is specific.\n\n- Say who you are and what you need: \"I am a first-year student in Kampala.\"\n- Give the task and the format: \"Explain budgeting in five short bullet points.\"\n- Add limits: audience, length, language.\n- Improve step by step: ask for a shorter version, a simpler example, or a different tone.\n\nVague request: \"Tell me about farming.\" Better request: \"List four low-cost ways a smallholder maize farmer can reduce post-harvest losses.\"',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(5,2,2,'reading','Checking what AI tells you','AI assistants can sound confident while being wrong. They sometimes invent facts, names, numbers and web links. This is often called a hallucination.\n\n## Check before you trust\n- Compare with a trusted source such as a government site, a university or a respected news organisation.\n- Double-check every number, date and quotation.\n- Ask the tool to explain its reasoning, then test that reasoning yourself.\n\nYou are responsible for what you submit or share, even if an AI helped you write it.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(6,2,3,'discussion','Improve a prompt','Write a vague request for an AI assistant, then rewrite it to be specific using the tips above. Post both versions in the discussion and say what you changed.',NULL,NULL,NULL,8,0),(7,3,1,'reading','Privacy and safety','Anything you type into an AI tool may be stored or used to improve the service. Treat it like a public notice board.\n\n## Never share\n- Passwords and PINs, including mobile money PINs\n- National ID, passport or bank numbers\n- Private photos or messages of other people\n- Confidential information from your school or employer\n\nIf you are unsure, remove names and details, or do not use the tool for that task.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(8,3,2,'reading','Bias, fairness and the future of work','AI learns from data created by people, so it can copy human unfairness. A hiring tool trained mostly on one group\'s past decisions may unfairly rank others lower.\n\n## Work is changing\nAI is likely to change many tasks rather than remove every job. People who combine their own knowledge with AI tools, check results carefully, communicate well and keep learning will stay valuable.',NULL,NULL,NULL,14,0),(9,3,3,'discussion','Where should humans decide?','Name one decision, for example approving a loan or marking an exam, where you believe a person should always make the final call, even if AI helps. Explain why.',NULL,NULL,NULL,8,0),(10,4,1,'reading','Passwords and two-step verification','A strong password is long, unique and hard to guess. A passphrase of several unrelated words, such as \"river-mango-clock-seven\", is easier to remember than random symbols and is stronger than a short complex password.\n\nUse a different password for every important account. Turn on two-step verification (a code sent to your phone or an app) for email, social media and money services. Even if someone learns your password, they still cannot get in.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(11,4,2,'reading','Spotting phishing and scams','Phishing messages try to trick you into sharing passwords, PINs or money.\n\n## Warning signs\n- Urgent threats: \"Your account will close today!\"\n- Requests for a PIN, password or one-time code\n- Unknown links or a sender address that looks slightly wrong\n- Offers that are too good to be true, such as a prize you never entered for\n\nA real bank or mobile money company will never ask for your PIN. When in doubt, do not click. Contact the company using the number you already know.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(12,4,3,'discussion','Share a scam story','Describe a scam message you or someone you know received (remove real names and numbers). What was the warning sign?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(13,5,1,'reading','Writing a professional email','A good work email is short, clear and polite.\n\n- Subject line: say what it is about, for example \"Request for meeting on Thursday\".\n- Greeting: \"Dear Ms Achieng,\" or \"Hello Peter,\".\n- Body: one main point per paragraph. State what you need and by when.\n- Close politely: \"Thank you\" and your full name.\n\nRead it once before sending. Check the recipient, the attachment and the tone.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(14,5,2,'reading','Documents in the cloud','Cloud tools let several people edit the same document and keep earlier versions. They also protect work if your phone or laptop is lost.\n\n## Good habits\n- Save important files online as well as on your device.\n- Share with specific people, not \"anyone with the link\", when the content is private.\n- Use comments and suggestions instead of sending many copies by email.\n- Name versions clearly, for example \"Budget_2026-03_v2\".',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(15,5,3,'discussion','Rewrite the email','Rewrite this weak message as a professional email: \"hi send me the report now\". Post your improved version.',NULL,NULL,NULL,8,0),(16,6,1,'reading','Organising and backing up files','Messy files waste time and risk loss. Create a few clear folders (for example Projects, Studies, Personal) and name files with a date and a short description.\n\nFollow the 3-2-1 idea in a simple form: keep your important work in at least two places, such as your device and an online account, and remember to update the copy regularly.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(17,6,2,'reading','Spreadsheet basics','A spreadsheet stores data in rows and columns. Formulas do the arithmetic for you.\n\n- =SUM(B2:B10) adds the numbers in cells B2 to B10.\n- =AVERAGE(B2:B10) gives the average.\n- =B2*C2 multiplies two cells, for example price times quantity.\n\nA chart makes numbers easier to understand. Use a column chart to compare amounts and a line chart to show change over time. Always label the chart and the axes.',NULL,NULL,NULL,15,0),(18,6,3,'discussion','What would you track?','Name one thing in your life or project you could track in a spreadsheet, such as savings, crop yields or attendance. What columns would you use?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(19,7,1,'reading','Facts, opinions and claims','A fact can be checked and shown to be true or false: \"The Nile flows through Uganda.\" An opinion is a personal view: \"The Nile is the most beautiful river.\" A claim is a statement that might be true and needs evidence: \"This herb cures malaria.\"\n\nCritical thinkers ask of every claim: who says so, what is the evidence, and what might they gain?',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(20,7,2,'reading','Mental shortcuts and bias','Our brains use shortcuts to decide quickly. They are useful but can mislead us.\n\n## Confirmation bias\nWe notice and remember information that agrees with what we already believe, and ignore what does not.\n\n## Emotional reasoning\nStrong feelings such as fear or anger make us share messages before checking them.\n\nSlow down when a message makes you feel very strong emotion. That is exactly when to check first.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(21,7,3,'discussion','A time I was wrong','Describe a time you believed something that turned out to be false. What made it feel believable, and how did you find out?',NULL,NULL,NULL,8,0),(22,8,1,'reading','The SIFT method','SIFT gives four quick moves for checking information.\n\n- Stop: pause before you react or share.\n- Investigate the source: who is behind this? Search their name and see what trusted sources say about them.\n- Find better coverage: look for the same claim in reliable news or official sites.\n- Trace claims to the original: find the study, photo or quotation the message refers to.\n\nOften two minutes is enough to learn that a viral message is false or missing context.',NULL,NULL,NULL,14,0),(23,8,2,'reading','Pictures, dates and context','Old photos and videos are often shared as if they were new. Before trusting an image, run a reverse image search to find where it first appeared. Check the date and place.\n\nAlso look for context. A real quotation can be shortened to change its meaning, and a real chart can have a misleading scale. Ask: what is missing?',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(24,8,3,'discussion','Check one claim','Pick a claim you have seen in a group chat or on social media. Use SIFT to check it, then report what you found and how long it took.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(25,9,1,'reading','Common reasoning mistakes','Some arguments look strong but are not.\n\n- Attacking the person: rejecting an idea because of who said it, not what was said.\n- False choice: \"Either we ban phones in class or learning fails.\" Usually there are more options.\n- Hasty generalisation: concluding about everyone from one or two cases.\n- Correlation is not causation: two things happening together does not prove one caused the other.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(26,9,2,'reading','Building a clear argument','A good argument has three parts: a claim, reasons, and evidence.\n\n1. State your claim in one sentence.\n2. Give two or three reasons.\n3. Support each reason with evidence such as data, an example, or a trusted source.\n4. Acknowledge the strongest opposing point and respond to it.\n\nKeep your tone respectful. The goal is to find what is true, not to win.',NULL,NULL,NULL,14,0),(27,9,3,'discussion','Spot the mistake','Write a short example of one reasoning mistake from this week and explain why it is weak. Read a classmate\'s example and say which mistake it shows.',NULL,NULL,NULL,8,0),(28,10,1,'reading','What is culture?','Culture is the shared habits, values and ways of doing things in a group. It includes language, greetings, humour, respect for elders, and attitudes to time and decisions.\n\nCulture is like an iceberg. A little is visible, such as food and clothing. Most is hidden, such as beliefs about respect, family and fairness. Misunderstandings usually come from the hidden part.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(29,10,2,'reading','Direct and indirect styles','In some groups people say exactly what they mean. In others, meaning is carried by tone, context and what is left unsaid, and saying \"no\" directly can seem rude.\n\nNeither style is better. When you work across cultures, do not assume that silence means agreement, or that a polite \"yes\" means the plan is confirmed. Ask open questions such as \"What worries you about this plan?\"',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(30,10,3,'discussion','A greeting from home','Describe how people usually greet each other where you live. What would a visitor need to know to greet someone respectfully?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(31,11,1,'reading','Plain language','Many East Africans speak two or three languages, and English may be a second or third language for some of your teammates. Plain language helps everyone.\n\n- Use short sentences and common words.\n- Avoid idioms such as \"hit the ground running\" and slang.\n- Explain abbreviations the first time.\n- Write one request per message and say clearly what you need and by when.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(32,11,2,'reading','Listening and checking understanding','Active listening means giving full attention and showing you understand.\n\n- Repeat back in your own words: \"So you will send the draft on Friday. Is that right?\"\n- Ask, do not assume: \"Can you tell me more about that?\"\n- Summarise decisions in writing after a call.\n\nSwahili is widely used across the region and learning a few greetings in your teammates\' languages shows respect.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(33,11,3,'discussion','Rewrite in plain language','Take a sentence full of idioms, such as \"Let\'s touch base offline and circle back,\" and rewrite it in plain language. Post both versions.',NULL,NULL,NULL,8,0),(34,12,1,'reading','Time zones and connectivity','Uganda, Kenya and Tanzania use East Africa Time (UTC+3). Rwanda and Burundi use Central Africa Time (UTC+2), one hour behind. A meeting at \"3 pm\" can easily be misunderstood, so always write the time zone and, if useful, both local times.\n\nNot everyone has fast internet. Offer an audio-only option, share slides in advance, keep files small, and record decisions in writing so people who miss a call can catch up.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(35,12,2,'reading','Repairing misunderstandings','Misunderstandings will happen. Handle them calmly.\n\n1. Assume good intent, then check facts.\n2. Describe what happened without blame: \"I understood the deadline was Monday.\"\n3. Ask how the other person saw it.\n4. Agree on what you will each do differently next time.\n\nA quick private message is often better than correcting someone in front of the group.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(36,12,3,'discussion','Plan a meeting','Your team has members in Kampala, Nairobi, Dar es Salaam and Kigali. Propose a meeting time, write it with both time zones, and list two things you would do to include people with weak connections.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(37,13,1,'reading','Goals and roles','Strong teams know where they are going and who does what.\n\n- A shared goal: one sentence everyone can repeat.\n- Clear roles: for example team lead, note-taker, researcher, and tester. Roles can rotate.\n- Complementary skills: the best teams mix disciplines. A farmer-turned-agronomist, a developer, a data analyst and an environmental scientist can solve problems none could solve alone.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(38,13,2,'reading','Team agreements','A team agreement is a short document written together at the start. It might say how often you will meet, which channel you use for urgent messages, how fast you will reply, and how you will make decisions.\n\nWriting rules early prevents arguments later. Review the agreement after a few weeks and improve it.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(39,13,3,'discussion','Best and worst team','Describe the best team you have been part of, and one thing that made it work. Then name one habit that harmed a team you were in.',NULL,NULL,NULL,8,0),(40,14,1,'reading','Tasks and milestones','A big project feels easier when it is split up.\n\n- A task is a small piece of work one person can finish, such as \"Interview three farmers.\"\n- A milestone is a major checkpoint made of several tasks, such as \"Prototype ready for testing.\"\n\nGive every task one owner and a due date. Many owners means no owner.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(41,14,2,'reading','Definition of done and simple boards','Agree what \"done\" means. \"Write the survey\" is done when it is reviewed by one teammate and tested with two people.\n\nA simple board with three columns, To do, In progress and Done, shows everyone the state of the work at a glance. Update it as you go. Small, frequent updates keep a remote team in sync.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(42,14,3,'discussion','Break it down','Pick a goal for a community project, such as setting up a school recycling programme. List five tasks and one milestone.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(43,15,1,'reading','Communication on a limited connection','Choose the lightest tool that does the job.\n\n- Short text messages for quick questions.\n- Written updates for decisions, so they are not lost.\n- Audio calls instead of video when bandwidth is weak.\n- Compress images and documents before sending, and avoid sending large files to everyone.\n\nWrite messages so they can be understood later without the whole chat history.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(44,15,2,'reading','Feedback and conflict','Feedback helps a team improve. Make it specific, kind and about the work, not the person.\n\nUse a simple pattern: what you observed, the effect it had, and what you suggest. For example: \"The report arrived after the deadline, so we could not review it. Could we agree to share drafts two days earlier?\"\n\nWhen conflict appears, talk early, listen first, focus on the problem, and ask a facilitator or mentor if you are stuck.',NULL,NULL,NULL,14,0),(45,15,3,'discussion','Give kind feedback','Rewrite this harsh comment as helpful feedback: \"Your slides are terrible.\" Post your version.',NULL,NULL,NULL,8,0),(46,16,1,'reading','Skills you already have','Skills come from jobs, school, volunteering, family responsibilities and projects. Technical skills are things like using spreadsheets or repairing phones. Transferable skills such as communication, problem solving, teamwork and reliability are useful in almost any job.\n\nExample: organising a church or community event shows planning, budgeting and teamwork.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(47,16,2,'reading','Setting career goals','Choose a direction before you apply. Ask yourself: What work do I enjoy? What does the local or regional market need? What skill should I build next?\n\nWrite one goal for six months and one for two years. Make them specific, for example \"Get an internship in data entry or analysis by June.\"',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(48,16,3,'discussion','Three strengths','List three strengths and one real example for each. Share one in the discussion and encourage a classmate by naming a strength you notice in their post.',NULL,NULL,NULL,8,0),(49,17,1,'reading','Structure of a strong CV','Keep your CV to one or two pages, in a clean layout with a readable font.\n\n- Contact details and a short profile (two or three lines).\n- Work, volunteering or project experience, newest first.\n- Education and training.\n- Skills and languages.\n\nUse action words and numbers: \"Organised a clean-up for 60 volunteers and collected 40 bags of waste.\" Check spelling twice.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(50,17,2,'reading','Tailoring and cover letters','Read the job advert carefully and use its key words where they honestly describe you. Change your profile to fit each role.\n\nA cover letter is three short paragraphs: why you are writing, why you fit the role with one or two examples, and a polite close. Address a named person if you can.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(51,17,3,'discussion','Improve a bullet point','Rewrite this weak CV line to include an action and a number: \"Helped at a shop.\" Post your version.',NULL,NULL,NULL,8,0),(52,18,1,'reading','The STAR method','Interviewers often ask for a time you did something, such as \"Tell me about a time you solved a problem.\" Use STAR to answer clearly.\n\n- Situation: the context in one or two sentences.\n- Task: what you needed to achieve.\n- Action: what YOU did, step by step.\n- Result: the outcome, with a number if possible, and what you learned.\n\nPractise aloud. Also prepare two questions to ask the employer, such as how success is measured in the role.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(53,18,2,'reading','Your professional online presence','Employers often search for candidates online. Use a professional photo, a clear headline and an accurate summary on a professional networking profile. Review what is public on social media and remove content you would not show an employer.\n\nBe careful with scams: a genuine employer will never ask you to pay for a job. Never send ID numbers or bank details before you have verified the company.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(54,18,3,'discussion','Practise a STAR story','Write a short STAR answer about a time you solved a problem or worked in a team. Share it and give one kind suggestion to a classmate.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(55,19,1,'reading','What is in your soil?','Soil is more than dirt. It holds minerals, air, water, tiny living organisms and organic matter (rotted plants and manure). Roots need all of these.\n\n## A simple field test\nTake a handful of moist soil and squeeze it. If it forms a ball that crumbles when poked, it is a good loam. If it stays sticky like clay, water drains slowly. If it falls apart like sand, it dries out fast.\n\nDark, crumbly soil that smells earthy usually has plenty of organic matter.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(56,19,2,'reading','Feeding the soil','Soil that is farmed without being fed gets tired.\n\n- **Compost and manure** add nutrients and help soil hold water.\n- **Mulch** (dry grass or crop residue on the surface) cools the soil, stops weeds and reduces evaporation.\n- **Legumes** such as beans and groundnuts add nitrogen when grown in rotation.\n- **Avoid bare soil**: rain washes it away. Keep it covered.\n\nBurning residues destroys organic matter. Mixing it back into the soil keeps it.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(57,19,3,'discussion','Your soil','Describe the soil where you live or farm (colour, texture, how it behaves after rain). What one change would you try first?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(58,20,1,'reading','Harvesting and saving water','Water is the biggest climate risk for most farms. The goal is to slow water down, spread it out and let it soak in.\n\n- **Planting pits and trenches** catch rain on dry land.\n- **Terraces and contour bunds** (low ridges along the slope) stop soil being washed downhill.\n- **Rooftop harvesting** stores rain in tanks for dry weeks.\n- **Mulch and shade** reduce losses from evaporation.\n\nCheapest first: mulch and planting along the contour cost almost nothing.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(59,20,2,'reading','Using water wisely','Water the root zone, not the leaves, and water early morning or late evening to reduce evaporation.\n\n## Drip and bottle systems\nA bottle with a small hole placed next to a seedling delivers water slowly. Simple drip kits use up to half the water of hand watering.\n\n## Too much rain\nHeavy rain needs drainage channels and covered soil. Plant grass strips along channels to slow the water.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(60,20,3,'discussion','Water on your land','Where does water go on a plot you know after a heavy storm? Suggest one way to slow it down.',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(61,21,1,'reading','Reading the season','Farmers have always watched the sky, and now there is more help: national weather services, radio forecasts and SMS advisory services give seasonal outlooks.\n\n- Plant when rains are established, not at the first shower.\n- Choose short-season or drought-tolerant varieties if the outlook is poor.\n- Stagger planting dates so one dry spell does not hit everything.\n\nKeep a simple notebook of planting dates, rainfall and yields. After two seasons it becomes your best forecast.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(62,21,2,'reading','Rotation and mixing crops','Growing the same crop on the same plot every year builds up pests and drains the soil.\n\n## Rotation\nFollow a cereal (maize, sorghum) with a legume (beans, cowpeas), then a root or vegetable crop.\n\n## Intercropping\nGrowing two crops together, such as maize with beans, uses space well, covers soil and spreads risk: if one fails, the other may still produce.\n\nDiversity is insurance.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(63,21,3,'discussion','Your crop mix','Which crops would you grow together or in rotation on a small plot, and why?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(64,22,1,'reading','Where losses happen','Food is lost at every step: harvesting too early or late, rough handling, slow drying, poor storage, pests and long delays to market.\n\nMost losses are preventable and many cost almost nothing to fix. Count the stages for your own crop and ask: where do we lose the most?',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(65,22,2,'reading','Drying grain and beans','Wet grain grows mould, and some moulds make toxins (aflatoxin) that are harmful to people and animals.\n\n- Harvest when the crop is mature and dry in the field if possible.\n- Dry on tarpaulins or raised racks, never directly on soil.\n- Turn the grain often and protect it from rain and animals.\n- Grain is dry enough when a kernel cracks with a snap when bitten or squeezed, or when a moisture meter reads about 13 percent for maize.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(66,22,3,'discussion','Losses you have seen','Tell us about food being lost after harvest in your community. What caused it?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(67,23,1,'reading','Storing grain','Clean, dry grain in a clean, dry store lasts much longer.\n\n- Clean the store and sacks before filling them.\n- Use hermetic (airtight) bags or sealed containers, which starve insects of oxygen without chemicals.\n- Stack sacks on pallets away from walls.\n- Check regularly for heat, damp smell or insect dust.\n\nNever mix new grain with old grain.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(68,23,2,'reading','Fresh fruit and vegetables','Fresh produce is alive and keeps losing water and quality after picking.\n\n- Harvest in the cool morning and keep produce in the shade.\n- Handle gently: bruises start rot.\n- Use clean, ventilated crates instead of heavy sacks.\n- Sort out damaged items so they do not spoil the rest.\n\nEvery hour of shade and every extra cushion of care is money saved.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(69,23,3,'discussion','Storage at home','How do people store grain or vegetables where you live? Which method would you improve and how?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(70,24,1,'reading','What is value addition?','Value addition means changing a product so it is worth more: cleaning, sorting, drying, grinding, cooking or packing.\n\nExamples: tomatoes to dried tomatoes or sauce, milk to yoghurt, cassava to flour, sunflower to cooking oil, bananas to dried chips.\n\nBefore you start, ask: who will buy it, at what price, and what does it cost me to make?',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(71,24,2,'reading','Clean, safe and well packed','Customers buy what looks clean and trustworthy.\n\n- Wash hands and equipment, and use clean water.\n- Keep raw and cooked foods apart.\n- Use clean, sealed packaging with a simple label: product name, date, your name and contact.\n- Start small, get feedback and check with the local standards or health office about any permits you need.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(72,24,3,'discussion','Your product idea','Pick a crop you know. What product could you make from it and who would buy it?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(73,25,1,'reading','Why keep records','If you do not write it down, you cannot know if you are making money. Records show which crops pay, where money leaks and what to change.\n\nA notebook is enough. Record: date, activity, money in, money out, quantities and who you sold to.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(74,25,2,'reading','Costs and gross margin','**Gross margin = income from sales minus the direct costs of producing it.**\n\nExample, one acre of beans:\n- Sales: 600 kg at 2,500 per kg = 1,500,000\n- Direct costs: seed, labour, fertiliser and transport = 900,000\n- Gross margin = 600,000\n\nCompare gross margins between crops to decide where to put your land and time. Do not forget your own labour: it has a value.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(75,25,3,'discussion','One crop, real numbers','Pick a crop you know. List its main costs and a rough price. Does it look profitable?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(76,26,1,'reading','Know your market','Different buyers pay different prices: roadside traders, local markets, schools and hotels, processors, supermarkets and exporters.\n\nCompare them on: price, quantity needed, quality required, payment time and transport cost. A slightly lower price paid on the spot can beat a higher price paid after three months.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(77,26,2,'reading','Negotiating and trust','Know your costs before you negotiate so you know your lowest acceptable price.\n\n- Ask what quality and quantity the buyer needs.\n- Agree price, date and payment method clearly, in writing if possible (a message is a record).\n- Deliver what you promised. Repeat customers are worth more than one big sale.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(78,26,3,'discussion','Where would you sell?','List three possible buyers for a crop you grow. Which would you pick and why?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(79,27,1,'reading','Why farmers group together','Alone, a small farmer has little bargaining power. Together, farmers can buy inputs in bulk, share transport, meet buyer volumes and gain training or loans.\n\nGood groups have clear rules, regular meetings, written records and shared leadership. Trust and transparency matter more than size.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(80,27,2,'reading','Savings and mobile money','Village savings groups (VSLAs) help members save regularly and borrow small loans.\n\nMobile money makes payments safer than carrying cash. Protect it: never share your PIN, confirm the recipient name before sending and keep transaction messages as records.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(81,27,3,'discussion','A group that works','Have you seen a farmers\' group or savings group succeed or fail? What made the difference?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(82,28,1,'reading','A good house','A house protects animals from rain, wind, heat, thieves and predators and keeps them dry.\n\n- Good ventilation but no draughts at animal level.\n- Dry floor with clean bedding (dry grass or wood shavings).\n- Enough space: crowded animals fight, stress and spread disease.\n- Easy to clean, with separate space for young animals.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(83,28,2,'reading','Feed and water','Animals need energy, protein, minerals and above all clean water. Layers and broilers need special feeds for their stage; goats and rabbits need forage plus a little supplement.\n\n- Change feed slowly, not suddenly.\n- Store feed dry; mouldy feed makes animals sick.\n- Clean drinkers daily. Water is the most important feed.\n\nFeed is usually 60 to 70 percent of the cost, so waste matters.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(84,28,3,'discussion','Your starter flock','If you started with 20 chickens or 5 goats, what would you need to prepare first?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(85,29,1,'reading','Biosecurity: keep disease out','Most diseases arrive on boots, hands, equipment, new animals and visitors.\n\n- Keep a footbath or change shoes at the door.\n- Isolate new or sick animals for at least two weeks.\n- Do not share equipment between farms without cleaning.\n- Remove dead birds and animals quickly and bury or burn them properly.\n\nPrevention is cheaper than treatment.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(86,29,2,'reading','Vaccination and early signs','Follow a vaccination schedule from a vet or extension officer. For chickens this usually includes Newcastle disease and Gumboro.\n\nWatch daily: animals that stay away from the group, stop eating, have watery droppings, difficulty breathing, swollen faces or sudden deaths need action. Isolate them and call a vet or extension officer quickly.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(87,29,3,'discussion','A health scare','Have you seen sick animals on a farm? What was done and what would you do differently?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(88,30,1,'reading','Simple records','Record every day or week: animals in and out, deaths, feed used, eggs or weight, vaccines and money spent or earned.\n\nFrom this you can calculate cost per egg or per kilogram and spot problems quickly: a sudden drop in eggs, for example, tells you something is wrong.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(89,30,2,'reading','Growing the business','Start small and learn before you expand. Reinvest profits step by step.\n\n- Know your selling price before you stock: eggs, live birds, manure and meat each have different markets.\n- Plan cash for feed until the first sales.\n- Sell regularly to trusted buyers: restaurants, schools, shops, neighbours.\n- Join a group to share vets, bulk feed purchases and transport.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(90,30,3,'discussion','Your first numbers','Estimate monthly feed cost and expected income for a small flock. Would it pay?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(91,31,1,'reading','Check, call, care','When something happens, follow three steps.\n\n1. **Check** the scene: is it safe for you? Do not become a second casualty.\n2. **Call** for help early. Know your local emergency number and the nearest health facility, and send someone to bring help.\n3. **Care** for the person with the skills below until help arrives.\n\nStay calm and speak clearly: where you are, what happened, how many people are hurt.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(92,31,2,'reading','Bleeding and burns','**Bleeding:** press firmly on the wound with a clean cloth and keep pressing. Raise the injured limb if possible. Do not remove the cloth if it soaks through; add more on top.\n\n**Burns:** cool the burn under clean running water for 20 minutes. Remove rings or tight clothing nearby before it swells. Cover loosely with a clean non-fluffy cloth. Do not use butter, toothpaste or ointments, and do not burst blisters. Seek medical care for large, deep or face burns.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(93,31,3,'discussion','Prepared at home?','What is in your home or school first aid kit, and what is missing? Share the nearest health facility number you would call.',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(94,32,1,'reading','Choking and fainting','**Choking:** if a person cannot cough, speak or breathe, give up to five firm back blows between the shoulder blades, then up to five abdominal thrusts. Call for help. Follow a trained provider\'s guidance for babies and small children.\n\n**Fainting:** lay the person down, raise their legs and loosen tight clothes. If they do not wake quickly or have trouble breathing, get urgent help.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(95,32,2,'reading','Fever, seizures and when to go to hospital','**Fever:** give fluids, rest and light clothing. Go to a health facility for very high fever, fever in babies or fever with stiff neck, confusion or rash. In malaria areas, get tested.\n\n**Seizures:** clear space, cushion the head, do not hold the person or put anything in the mouth, and time it. After it stops, place them on their side. Call for help if it lasts more than five minutes or happens repeatedly.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(96,32,3,'discussion','Myths and facts','Which first aid myths have you heard (for example putting things in the mouth during a seizure)? What is the safer advice?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(97,33,1,'reading','Hands, water and food','Many illnesses, including diarrhoea and cholera, spread through dirty hands, unsafe water and unclean food.\n\n- Wash hands with soap and water after the toilet and before eating or preparing food.\n- Treat drinking water if unsure: boiling, chlorine tablets or safe filters.\n- Cook food thoroughly, cover it, and keep raw and cooked foods apart.\n- Use a clean latrine and keep it away from water sources.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(98,33,2,'reading','Sharing health messages','People listen to people they trust. When sharing health information:\n\n- Use simple words in the local language.\n- Show, not only tell: demonstrate handwashing.\n- Listen first and respect beliefs.\n- Use trusted sources (the Ministry of Health, WHO, local clinics) and say when you are not sure.\n- Never spread rumours or share private health information.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(99,33,3,'discussion','Your community','What one health habit would make the biggest difference in your community and how would you share it?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(100,34,1,'reading','Everyone has a mind to look after','Mental health means how we think, feel and cope. Like physical health, it goes up and down. Feeling worried before an exam is normal. It becomes a concern when feelings are strong, last a long time or stop you from living your life.\n\nAsking for help is a sign of strength, and mental health problems are common and treatable.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(101,34,2,'reading','Signs and small tools','**Common signs:** trouble sleeping, constant worry, feeling hopeless, changes in appetite, withdrawing from friends, irritability, or loss of interest.\n\n**Small tools that help:**\n- Slow breathing: in for four, out for six, a few times.\n- Move: a walk or dancing lifts mood.\n- Write worries down, then pick one small step.\n- Talk to someone you trust.\n- Keep regular sleep and meals.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(102,34,3,'discussion','What helps you','What do you do when you feel stressed that really helps? Share one idea others can try.',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(103,35,1,'reading','Sleep, body and routine','Poor sleep makes worry louder. Aim for regular sleep and wake times, avoid screens just before bed and limit caffeine late in the day.\n\nEat regular meals, drink water and move your body. A routine gives the day structure, which helps when life feels uncertain.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(104,35,2,'reading','Connection and social media','Real connection protects mental health: friends, family, faith groups, clubs and sports.\n\nSocial media can inform and connect, but constant comparison hurts. Remember that people post highlights, not whole lives. Curate your feed, take breaks, switch off notifications at night and never share cruel messages or private images.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(105,35,3,'discussion','Online habits','What is one change you could make to feel better about your time online?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(106,36,1,'reading','How to listen','When a friend is struggling:\n\n- Find a private moment and ask: \"How are you really doing?\"\n- Listen without interrupting or judging.\n- Say: \"Thank you for telling me. That sounds hard.\"\n- Do not promise to keep secrets if their safety is at risk.\n- Encourage them to talk to a trusted adult or professional and offer to go with them.\n\nYou are a friend, not a counsellor.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(107,36,2,'reading','When to get help','Seek help urgently if someone talks about wanting to die or hurt themselves, or if they cannot cope. Contact emergency services, a health facility, a school counsellor or a national helpline in your country, and stay with the person.\n\nFor ongoing worries, a counsellor, nurse or doctor can guide you. You do not need to wait until it is very bad.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(108,36,3,'discussion','Reducing stigma','What words or attitudes make it hard to ask for help? What would make it easier?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(109,37,1,'reading','The parts of a solar system','A basic solar home system has four parts.\n\n- **Solar panel:** turns sunlight into electricity during the day.\n- **Charge controller:** protects the battery from overcharging.\n- **Battery:** stores energy for night and cloudy days.\n- **Loads:** lights, phone chargers, radios or a TV.\n\nPanels must face the sun, be kept clean and not be shaded by trees or roofs.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(110,37,2,'reading','Watts, hours and watt-hours','**Power (watts)** tells you how fast a device uses energy. **Energy (watt-hours)** is power multiplied by hours used.\n\nExample: a 5 W LED bulb used 4 hours a day uses 20 Wh per day. Add all devices to get your daily need, then choose a panel and battery big enough, with some spare for cloudy days.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(111,37,3,'discussion','Power at home','Which devices do you or your neighbours use most? Are they efficient?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(112,38,1,'reading','Quality and safety','Not all solar products are equal. Look for quality-verified products (for example the Lighting Global / VeraSol mark), a clear warranty and local after-sales support.\n\nSafety: use correct cables, do not mix old and new batteries, keep batteries dry and ventilated and have a qualified technician install larger systems.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(113,38,2,'reading','Costs and pay-as-you-go','A solar kit costs more upfront than kerosene but saves money over time. Pay-as-you-go (PAYG) lets customers pay in small mobile-money instalments until they own the system.\n\nCompare: total price, deposit, instalment size, warranty and what happens if you miss a payment. Calculate how much you currently spend on kerosene, charging and batteries to see the payback time.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(114,38,3,'discussion','Compare the cost','How much does a household you know spend each month on light and phone charging? Would solar pay back?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(115,39,1,'reading','Why clean cooking matters','Cooking over open fires fills homes with smoke that harms lungs, especially for women and children, and uses a lot of wood or charcoal.\n\nImproved cookstoves burn less fuel and make less smoke. Other options include LPG, biogas and electric pressure cookers where power is affordable. Saving fuel saves money, time spent collecting wood and forests.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(116,39,2,'reading','Green energy jobs for young people','Opportunities include selling and installing solar kits, repairing devices, running phone-charging services, making and selling cookstoves, collecting and recycling batteries safely and training others.\n\nStart with a licensed company\'s agent programme or a cooperative. Learn about safety, customer service and records before investing.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(117,39,3,'discussion','Your energy idea','Which clean energy business could work in your area and what would you need to start?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(118,40,1,'reading','Problems, not just ideas','The best businesses solve a real problem for real people. Instead of asking \"What can I sell?\", ask \"What do people around me struggle with or spend money on?\"\n\nList five problems you see. For each, who has it, how do they solve it today and would they pay for a better way?',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(119,40,2,'reading','Test before you invest','Before you spend money, talk to ten possible customers.\n\n- Ask what they do now, what annoys them and what they pay.\n- Show a sample or offer a small trial.\n- See if anyone pays or pre-orders.\n\nA small test, such as selling to ten people for a week, teaches you more than a long plan. Change your idea based on what you hear.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(120,40,3,'discussion','Your problem list','Share one local problem you could solve and who would pay for it.',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(121,41,1,'reading','Pricing','Your price must cover your costs and leave profit.\n\n**Price = cost per unit + profit margin.**\n\nExample: it costs 1,200 to make a chapati meal and you want 30 percent profit: price at about 1,560, then check what customers will pay and what competitors charge.\n\nDo not forget your own time, transport, packaging and losses.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(122,41,2,'reading','Cash and records','Many businesses with sales still fail because they run out of cash.\n\n- Separate business money from personal money.\n- Write down every sale and expense daily.\n- Keep a small reserve.\n- Pay yourself a fixed small amount instead of taking cash whenever you want.\n\nAt the end of the week add up: sales minus expenses equals profit.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(123,41,3,'discussion','Price check','Choose a product you could sell. What does it cost to make and what price would you charge?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(124,42,1,'reading','Finding and keeping customers','Start where your customers already are: neighbours, markets, WhatsApp groups, schools and churches.\n\n- Make it easy to buy and pay (mobile money).\n- Deliver on time and as promised.\n- Ask for feedback and referrals.\n- Thank customers and remember them.\n\nA happy customer tells others for free.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(125,42,2,'reading','The one-page plan','Write on one page: the problem, your product, your customers, your price, your costs, how you will reach customers, how much money you need and the first three things you will do this month.\n\nShare it with a mentor or a friend and ask what is unclear. A plan is a living tool and you update it as you learn.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(126,42,3,'discussion','Your first ten customers','Where will you find your first ten customers? What will you say to them?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(127,43,1,'reading','What makes a story','A story has a person, a problem or goal, and a change. \"A young farmer lost half her tomatoes, then learned to dry them and now sells to a school\" is a story. \"Farming is hard\" is a statement.\n\nBefore filming, answer: who is it for, what do I want them to feel or do, and who is the main person?',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(128,43,2,'reading','Plan with a storyboard','Sketch six to eight simple pictures on paper: the opening shot, the problem, the action, the result and the closing line.\n\nPlan the shots you need: wide (the place), medium (the person) and close-up (hands, faces, details). A plan saves time and battery.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(129,43,3,'discussion','Your story idea','Share a short story idea in your community and who it is for.',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(130,44,1,'reading','Photos and video basics','- **Light:** face the light, not away from it. Soft morning or evening light is flattering.\n- **Steady:** hold the phone with two hands or rest it on something. Film in landscape for most uses.\n- **Frame:** place your subject slightly off-centre and leave space; keep the background tidy.\n- **Clean the lens** with a soft cloth.\n- Record each shot for ten seconds or more so you have material to edit.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(131,44,2,'reading','Sound matters','Bad sound ruins good pictures. Record in a quiet place, bring the phone close to the speaker (about a hand\'s length) and avoid wind and traffic.\n\nTest first and listen with earphones. A cheap clip-on microphone helps but is not required.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,0),(132,44,3,'discussion','Shot of the week','Describe one shot you will capture this week and how you will set it up.',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(133,45,1,'reading','Editing simply','Free apps (such as CapCut, InShot or a phone\'s built-in editor) let you trim clips, add titles and captions, and mix sound.\n\n- Keep it short: 60 to 90 seconds is enough for most stories.\n- Cut out the boring parts.\n- Add captions so people can watch without sound.\n- Check volume and spelling before exporting.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(134,45,2,'reading','Consent, dignity and sharing','Always ask permission before filming or posting people, and be extra careful with children. Explain where it will be shared and let them say no.\n\n- Show people with dignity, not as victims.\n- Do not share private places or sensitive information.\n- Credit music and images or use free-to-use ones.\n- After posting, look at views, comments and shares to learn what worked.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(135,45,3,'discussion','Your ethics check','What would you ask permission for before sharing your story? How would you ask?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(136,46,1,'reading','Start with a question','Data is only useful if it answers a question. \"Which of my three vegetables earns the most per week?\" is a good question. \"Collect everything\" is not.\n\nWrite your question, decide what you need to measure (price, quantity, date), and how often.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(137,46,2,'reading','Collecting well','- Use the same units every time (kilograms, not sometimes sacks).\n- Record date, place and who collected it.\n- Use a form or a table with fixed columns.\n- Collect from enough cases: ten records are better than two.\n- Ask permission when collecting information about people and keep it safe.\n\nMessy collection makes analysis painful later.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(138,46,3,'discussion','Your question','Share a question you would like data to answer in your farm, shop or project.',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(139,47,1,'reading','Tidy tables','A tidy spreadsheet has one row per record and one column per measurement, with a clear heading at the top.\n\nClean by fixing spelling differences (\"Kampala\" and \"kampala \"), removing duplicates, filling or flagging missing values and checking impossible numbers (for example a price of 0 or a tomato weighing 500 kg).',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,1),(140,47,2,'reading','Totals and averages','In any spreadsheet:\n- `=SUM(B2:B10)` adds numbers.\n- `=AVERAGE(B2:B10)` gives the mean.\n- `=MAX` and `=MIN` find the highest and lowest.\n\nAn average hides extremes: five days of 10 sales and one of 100 gives an average of 25, which describes none of the days. Look at the whole list too.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(141,47,3,'discussion','Messy data','What problems have you seen in lists or records (duplicates, different spellings, missing values)?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0),(142,48,1,'reading','Choosing a chart','- **Bar chart:** compare categories (sales by product).\n- **Line chart:** show change over time (rainfall by month).\n- **Pie chart:** parts of a whole, with few slices.\n\nGive every chart a clear title, labels and units. Keep it simple: one message per chart.',NULL,NULL,NULL,10,1),(143,48,2,'reading','Honest charts and privacy','Charts can mislead: an axis that does not start at zero makes small differences look huge, cherry-picked dates hide the full story and missing labels confuse readers.\n\nBe honest: show the full range, say where the data came from and avoid sharing names or private details. Anonymise data about people whenever possible.',NULL,NULL,NULL,12,0),(144,48,3,'discussion','Spot the trick','Have you seen a chart that seemed to exaggerate something? What was misleading?',NULL,NULL,NULL,6,0);
